Code statements:
```c
if (READ_ONCE(msk->snd_data_fin_enable) && !(ssk->sk_shutdown & SEND_SHUTDOWN)) {
    gfp_t saved_allocation = ssk->sk_allocation;

    /* we are in a atomic (BH) scope, override ssk default for data
     * fin allocation
     */
    ssk->sk_allocation = GFP_ATOMIC;
    ssk->sk_shutdown |= SEND_SHUTDOWN;
    tcp_shutdown(ssk, SEND_SHUTDOWN);
    ssk->sk_allocation = saved_allocation;
}
```

Code statements:
```c
if (READ_ONCE(msk->snd_data_fin_enable) && !(ssk->sk_shutdown & SEND_SHUTDOWN)) {
    gfp_t saved_allocation = ssk->sk_allocation;

    /* we are in a atomic (BH) scope, override ssk default for data
     * fin allocation
     */
    ssk->sk_allocation = GFP_ATOMIC;
    ssk->sk_shutdown |= SEND_SHUTDOWN;
    tcp_shutdown(ssk, SEND_SHUTDOWN);
    ssk->sk_allocation = saved_allocation;
}
```

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (READ_ONCE(msk->snd_data_fin_enable) && !(ssk->sk_shutdown & SEND_SHUTDOWN)) {
    ...
    tcp_shutdown(ssk, SEND_SHUTDOWN);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the fallback mechanism does not check if the MPTCP socket (`ssk`) has been shut down before attempting to close the TCP connection. If the socket is already shut down, the `tcp_shutdown` function may not be called, leading to a resource leak or undefined behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (READ_ONCE(msk->snd_data_fin_enable) && !(ssk->sk_shutdown & SEND_SHUTDOWN)) {
    ...
    tcp_shutdown(ssk, SEND_SHUTDOWN);
}
```
Reason:
The patch explicitly checks the shutdown state of the MPTCP socket (`ssk`) before calling `tcp_shutdown`. This ensures that the TCP connection is only closed if the socket is not already shut down, preventing the race condition and ensuring proper resource management.

